From e08630c0df8d5e4f3e63d691598c5791acbd0f5d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Francesco Pham <francesco.pham1@h-partners.com>
Date: Tue, 21 Jul 2026 20:01:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] graphic_2d: accept RS transactions when binder can't
 supply the caller pid

On the hybris_generic port OHOS runs on the Volla X23's Android/Halium
kernel binder. RenderService's CommitTransaction is a oneway (TF_ASYNC)
binder call, and on this kernel the async path does not deliver the sender
pid to the receiver: RSClientToServiceConnectionStub::OnRemoteRequest sees
GetCallingPid() == 0 for every async commit. The calling *token* is still
delivered (it is carried in the parcel, not the binder transaction header),
so isNonSystemAppCalling is still classified correctly.

RSTransactionData::IsCallingPidValid rejects any command whose owning pid
(ExtractPid(nodeId)) does not match the binder calling pid, unless the node
is a UIExtension surface. With callingPid == 0 this never matches for a
non-system app, so every command is marked invalid and skipped in
Process() -- the app's surface/root/attach/content commands never execute
on the server. The check is gated behind isNonSystemAppCalling, so system
apps (all of /system/app: launcher, sceneboard, Notes, ...) bypass it and
render normally, which masked the problem. The one non-system app in a
default image is the sample IME (com.example.kikakeyboard): its panel
surface stayed DEFAULT/childless on the server and composited an empty
buffer, so the on-screen keyboard bound and hit-tested correctly but never
drew. Any third-party (non-system) app would fail to render the same way.

When binder cannot supply the caller identity (callingPid == 0), fall back
to the pid the client stamps into the transaction payload via
SetSendingPid(GetRealPid()) -- the same value RS already trusts to route
transactions (RSUnmarshalThread cachedTransactionDataMap_). The fallback
only engages when callingPid == 0, so validation is unchanged on a normal
OHOS kernel that reports the caller pid for oneway calls.

Deeper cause: the Android/Halium binder driver this port boots on does not
deliver sender_pid for oneway transactions the way the OHOS kernel binder
does. Any code relying on IPCSkeleton::GetCallingPid() over a oneway call
observes 0 here; synchronous calls and the calling token are unaffected. A
device-side diagnostic confirmed the 0 is universal -- system apps' async
commits see it too -- not specific to the IME; the IME was merely the only
non-system app subject to the IsCallingPidValid gate. This is consistent
with the keyboard rendering on the OHOS-kernel emulator (which delivers the
oneway pid) but not on-device.

Change-Id: Ifc886dfaaab42c0b31fcbf13fda56f08063b94db
Signed-off-by: Francesco Pham <francesco.pham1@h-partners.com>
---
 .../src/transaction/rs_transaction_data.cpp            | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/rosen/modules/render_service_base/src/transaction/rs_transaction_data.cpp b/rosen/modules/render_service_base/src/transaction/rs_transaction_data.cpp
index c2bbb54207..829ab2fd75 100644
--- a/rosen/modules/render_service_base/src/transaction/rs_transaction_data.cpp
+++ b/rosen/modules/render_service_base/src/transaction/rs_transaction_data.cpp
@@ -364,6 +364,14 @@ bool RSTransactionData::UnmarshallingCommand(Parcel& parcel)
 
 bool RSTransactionData::IsCallingPidValid(pid_t callingPid, const RSRenderNodeMap& nodeMap) const
 {
+    // hybris_generic port: OHOS runs on the Volla X23's Android/Halium-kernel binder, where the
+    // async (TF_ASYNC) CommitTransaction path can surface GetCallingPid() == 0 on the RS server even
+    // for a genuine cross-process commit (the binder caller identity is not delivered for oneway here,
+    // though the calling token still is). When binder cannot supply the caller pid, fall back to the
+    // pid the client stamped into the transaction payload (SetSendingPid) -- the same value RS already
+    // trusts for routing (RSUnmarshalThread cachedTransactionDataMap_). Only engaged when callingPid == 0,
+    // so strict validation is unchanged on kernels that report the caller pid correctly.
+    const pid_t effectiveCallingPid = (callingPid == 0) ? pid_ : callingPid;
     std::unordered_map<pid_t, std::unordered_map<NodeId, std::set<
         std::pair<uint16_t, uint16_t>>>> inaccessibleCommandMap;
     std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(commandMutex_);
@@ -374,7 +382,7 @@ bool RSTransactionData::IsCallingPidValid(pid_t callingPid, const RSRenderNodeMa
         const NodeId nodeId = command->GetNodeId();
         const pid_t commandPid = ExtractPid(nodeId);
         bool allowNonSystemAppCalling = command->GetAccessPermission() != RSCommandPermissionType::PERMISSION_SYSTEM;
-        if (allowNonSystemAppCalling && (callingPid == commandPid || nodeMap.IsUIExtensionSurfaceNode(nodeId))) {
+        if (allowNonSystemAppCalling && (effectiveCallingPid == commandPid || nodeMap.IsUIExtensionSurfaceNode(nodeId))) {
             continue;
         }
         inaccessibleCommandMap[commandPid][nodeId].insert(command->GetUniqueType());
